From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Norman Herms <dockseed@proton.me>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 06:24:14 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: allwinner: a733: cubie-a7s: Enable Ethernet

The Cubie A7S has one RJ45 port, driven by GMAC0 through a Maxio
MAE0621A PHY at MDIO address 1. It has no network in Linux.

Enable GMAC0 with the RGMII pins and describe the PHY, supplied by
SWOUT1. The RGMII pins are in bank PH, whose I/O supply is SWOUT2 at
3.3 V; name it, so that the pin controller sets the bank's voltage from
the real rail.

The PHY reset is PH16, active low. The pin rests low, so the PHY does not
answer on MDIO until the reset is released. The reset is therefore on the
MDIO bus, which releases it before the bus is scanned.

The board has no delay lines, so the mode is rgmii-id. The PHY cannot
add the delays: it has no delay register, and the RX delay strap in the
schematic does not take effect on the silicon, as the RX window found
from the MAC side is centred on the delay RGMII asks for. The MAC adds
both delays instead.

Not covered: a driver for the PHY, which runs on the generic one.

Tested on Radxa Cubie A7S: booted on two boards; the generic PHY driver
finds the PHY (0x7b744412) at address 1, the link comes up at 1000/full,
also after a down and up, 20 of 20 pings return, and iperf3 runs in
both directions without retransmissions or errors.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 .../dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts   | 33 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 33 insertions(+)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
index dd6dec6411d6..0e5b82aec3dd 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s.dts
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@ / {
 	compatible = "radxa,cubie-a7s", "allwinner,sun60i-a733";
 
 	aliases {
+		ethernet0 = &gmac0;
 		serial0 = &uart0;
 		mmc0 = &mmc0;
 		mmc2 = &mmc2;
@@ -75,6 +76,34 @@ reg_vcc3v3: vcc3v3 {
 	};
 };
 
+&gmac0 {
+	pinctrl-names = "default";
+	pinctrl-0 = <&rgmii0_ph_pins>;
+	phy-handle = <&ext_rgmii_phy>;
+	/*
+	 * No delay lines on the board. The PHY adds no delay: it has no
+	 * delay register, and the RX delay strap in the schematic does not
+	 * take effect on the silicon. The MAC adds both delays.
+	 */
+	phy-mode = "rgmii-id";
+	phy-supply = <&reg_swout1>;
+	tx-internal-delay-ps = <1260>;
+	rx-internal-delay-ps = <1800>;
+	status = "okay";
+};
+
+&mdio0 {
+	/* PH16 rests low, so the PHY is held in reset until released */
+	reset-gpios = <&pio 7 16 GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW>;
+	reset-delay-us = <10000>;
+	reset-post-delay-us = <100000>;
+
+	ext_rgmii_phy: ethernet-phy@1 {
+		compatible = "ethernet-phy-ieee802.3-c22";
+		reg = <1>;
+	};
+};
+
 &mmc0 {
 	vmmc-supply = <&reg_vcc3v3>;
 	cd-gpios = <&pio 5 6 (GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH | GPIO_PULL_DOWN)>; /* PF6 */
@@ -100,6 +129,10 @@ &osc {
 	clock-frequency = <26000000>;
 };
 
+&pio {
+	vcc-ph-supply = <&reg_swout2>;
+};
+
 &r_i2c0 {
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	pinctrl-0 = <&r_i2c0_pl_pins>;
